RAJEEV MAHAJAN: Thanks.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.770
So whenever you
think about migrating

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:57.050
from on premises clusters
to Google Cloud Platform,

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:00.140
there are three aspects
that you have to consider--

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.447
migrating your infrastructure,
your data, and your jobs.

00:01:04.447 --> 00:01:06.488
And that's what we are
going to talk about today.

00:01:06.488 --> 00:01:06.987
OK.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.470
There are a few recommendations
we are going to make,

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.040
and we are going to stress
them over and over again

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.900
in this presentation,
and they are as follows.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.770
First, when you are thinking
about infrastructure,

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:27.230
we highly encourage you to look
at other Google Cloud Platform

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:32.570
services that will really make
running your clusters very

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.340
economical and very efficient.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.350
Second, we want you to
consider migrating from HDFS

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.970
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.120
And we'll talk
about why and how.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.470
Third, we want to,
again, encourage

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:52.200
you to consider something
called ephemeral clusters.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.330
Ephemeral clusters are
job specific clusters,

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:01.200
as contrasted to general
purpose clusters that

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.950
run multiple jobs in a
shared cluster environment.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.140
So we want to encourage
you to think about those,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.330
and we are again going
to talk a lot about them.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.466
So that's the general
outline of this presentation.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.370
Let's get the most important
things out of the way very,

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.590
very quickly.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.320
What are the benefits of
migrating to Google Cloud

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.860
Platform?

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:29.480
And consider the fact that I'm
saying Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:33.580
I am not saying
Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.310
There is a reason for that.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.450
So let's see what that means.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.030
First, Google Cloud
Dataproc, which

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:45.470
is what you will be migrating
to, is a cloud service.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:51.500
So you get pay as you go and
you get vast and varied scale.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.160
Make sense?

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.720
Second, it is a managed service.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:02.160
We provision the VMs for you, we
provision the clusters for you,

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.830
we deploy operating
systems on them,

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:10.900
and we deploy the open source
stack on these nodes, which

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.760
means that you
don't have to do it

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:15.250
and you don't have to carry
the administrative overhead

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:16.970
to do it.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:22.820
Third, if you think
about Cloud Dataproc,

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:27.260
that is Google's implementation
of the open source Hadoop

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.610
infrastructure.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:35.120
Let me define Cloud Dataproc
as we talk about it internally.

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.430
Cloud Dataproc is
a managed service

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.220
to run your Spark
and Hadoop jobs.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.080
That's what it is.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.010
If you're trying to migrate from
your on-premise clusters, which

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.640
are running that
open source stack,

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.370
it's very easy to
do that by just

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.430
making some minor
modifications to your job core.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.290
If that's all you
are looking to do--

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:04.990
you are focused on a lift and
shift strategy right now--

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:07.180
it's very simple to do that.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.365
But I think where you get value
is when you start implementing

00:04:11.365 --> 00:04:13.940
our recommendations that
we've just talked about,

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:17.620
and I am going to kind of
elaborate on what that value

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:18.430
proposition is.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.870
OK.

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:23.150
The next two-- four and five--

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.775
really are about
Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:25.775 --> 00:04:29.060
They are not about
Google Cloud Dataproc.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:36.950
We just talked about running
ephemeral clusters, which

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:41.400
are job specific
clusters, and what

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.000
we are trying to stress there
is rather than deploying

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.360
a whole bunch of libraries
for running Hadoop and Spark

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.600
and Hive and whatever else
that you can think of,

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.340
we just want you to focus
on the job of interest.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.280
That means you do not have
library clashes, et cetera,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:00.090
et cetera.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:03.540
The management overhead
is low and it really

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.550
greases the wheels of your
operations really, really well.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:10.010
Make sense?

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:15.802
Finally, Hadoop
has a blind spot,

00:05:15.802 --> 00:05:20.470
and the blind spot is
when you are working

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:24.280
with persistent
data, it is in HDFS

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.580
and it is not very
easy to go and unlock

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:30.730
the value of that data and
move it to other platforms

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:33.670
where the value
really gets used.

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:38.380
Let me establish a
very simple use case.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.720
Say you just ran a Spark
job and the Spark job

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.760
was creating recommendations for
powering your personalization

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.040
engine on your website.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.920
How do you get that data
that your Spark job just

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.610
generated, which is
stored in HDFS, out

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:58.780
to your recommendation engine?

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:02.950
That is a challenge, and
we, with our whole plethora

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:07.450
of different services,
actually do that really well.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.790
And we are going to
talk about this--

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.332
a couple of
different points, OK.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:15.089
Let's get started.

00:06:15.089 --> 00:06:16.630
Section number 1,
just-- you're going

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:18.142
to focus on infrastructure.

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:24.350
Let's step back.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:27.466
Let's look at our
on-premise clusters.

00:06:27.466 --> 00:06:29.550
There are three
characteristics which stand out

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:31.650
for these on-premise clusters.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:36.630
One, they're monolithic and they
have a large number of nodes

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.480
that you need to make
sure get upgraded

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.090
and have the right
software in tandem.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.310
If you don't do that,
you'll have trouble,

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:46.740
and you know all that.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.930
Second, these are persistent.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.080
They run for many
days, which means

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.840
it leads to other kinds
of management overhead

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.420
with making sure that
everything-- all the job

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:01.500
configuration is right and
there are no clashes and nothing

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:04.396
has died and everything is up.

00:07:04.396 --> 00:07:11.400
Third, these clusters are shared
across jobs, teams, projects,

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:13.170
and that sharing
infrastructure leads

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.450
to a whole bunch of
different problems

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:19.489
that you are probably
very aware of.

00:07:19.489 --> 00:07:20.780
I'm not going to focus on that.

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:23.029
We're going to talk about
how to solve these problems.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.790
The key characteristic
which creates

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:33.240
a lot of problems with
on-premise clusters

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:39.180
is the fact that they are
persistent and they are shared.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:44.360
And we just talked about
data being locked in HDFS,

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.410
and we need to figure
out a way to unlock that.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:47.960
So that's one limitation.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.680
Second, HDFS, because of
its replication factor--

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.770
2, 3, 4-- I don't know what
you guys are running with--

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:57.300
it's expensive.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:02.820
Third, if you are not using
Google Cloud Platform,

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.890
you have an opportunity cost.

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:06.430
What is that opportunity cost?

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:10.890
You cannot use other services
that can help unlock the value

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.446
inside your clusters.

00:08:13.446 --> 00:08:14.940
Make sense?

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:18.120
Let's look at some
solutions now.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.430
Very simple diagram--
first of many.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:25.550
Your on-premise
cluster is on the left

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:31.130
and your migrated
clusters are on the right.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:34.820
On-premise, you are running
two clusters, four jobs,

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.330
and two HDFS setups.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:40.520
When you are done
migrating, you will actually

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:43.159
have three clusters--

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:45.830
job three and four actually
get their own clusters.

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:49.280
Remember, I've been talking
about ephemeral clusters, which

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:53.540
are job specific, which
means one cluster per job.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:56.630
So I got three clusters
running and all my data

00:08:56.630 --> 00:09:01.450
is stored in Google
Cloud Storage buckets.

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:06.210
So remember, I said
migrate from HDFS to GCS.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:07.840
Use ephemeral clusters.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:09.360
This is that architecture.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.530
Very simple.

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:11.270
Basic.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:16.600
We are going to build on this,
but this is our starting point.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:20.860
Now before we actually
delve even deeper,

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:23.310
let's talk about
two very important

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:28.260
architectural principles that
are embedded in Google Cloud

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.700
Dataproc, and our
migration recommendations

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:36.226
aligned really, really well with
those architectural principles.

00:09:36.226 --> 00:09:37.600
The first
architectural principle

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.200
is separate your data
from computation.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:44.600
Now this particular
architectural principle

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:48.490
is not unique to Cloud Dataproc.

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:52.510
Pretty much all of
Google's big data platform

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:53.590
and different offerings--

00:09:53.590 --> 00:09:55.750
BigQuery et cetera--
they are built

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:59.280
on this architectural principle.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:03.090
Now how that translates
into specific things

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.250
you need to do with Cloud
Dataproc-- that follows.

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:09.010
First, we are asking you to
migrate from HDFS to GCS.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:10.330
That's your separation.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:12.100
Separate the data
out from the cluster.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:15.060
Remove it from HDFS.

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:19.570
GCS is HDFS
compliant, which means

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:21.811
that it's only slight
modifications you need

00:10:21.811 --> 00:10:23.560
to make to your code,
and we will actually

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:28.780
show you what those are and you
are running in Cloud Dataproc.

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:32.200
But as I said earlier, if
all you are looking to do

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:35.240
is to do a lift and shift,
it's a very simple thing.

00:10:35.240 --> 00:10:37.420
But if you trying
to get value, we

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:40.860
will go and talk
about other things.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:48.590
Second, GCS is faster, GCS is
definitely more economical,

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:53.030
and GCS is very easy to manage--

00:10:53.030 --> 00:10:56.340
little to no
management overhead.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.620
And we already
talked about the fact

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:03.570
that if you are looking to
unlock value within HDFS,

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.640
and if you move
to GCS, the way it

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:09.180
works is most of
Google Cloud Platform

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:12.430
technologies work very
seamlessly with Google Cloud

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:16.180
Storage, which means
that is your venue.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:18.600
That is your door
to opening the value

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:23.640
from within Cloud from
your on-premise clusters

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:25.500
in Cloud Dataproc.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:27.180
Make sense?

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:29.420
Let's talk about the second
architectural principle,

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.310
which I've already
talked about three times

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.710
and I'm going to talk
about 10 times more.

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:37.740
Use ephemeral clusters.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:46.910
Now, if you have jobs sharing
the same infrastructure,

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:51.440
you have to do a setup
before every job run.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:53.600
You have to make sure
that the configuration

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:56.540
for the cluster for
that particular job run

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.400
is set up right.

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:00.560
That's quite a bit of
management overhead.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.680
If I actually am
now running clusters

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.480
which are job specific,
I don't have to do that.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.900
I just have to configure
that particular job.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:14.350
I also don't have to
deploy a whole bunch

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:16.850
of different libraries.

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:21.160
So if I'm running a Spark job,
I don't need to deploy Hadoop

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:24.440
libraries or Hive libraries.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:25.510
Make sense?

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:28.060
Easier to manage.

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:33.630
The easiest analogy I can
think about this architectural

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:36.800
principle is that old
proverb that you've heard.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:41.030
Fire is a very poor
master, but a great slave,

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:43.020
or some variation of that.

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:44.990
Clusters, because of
all the limitations

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:49.460
we talked about earlier, are
actually running you ragged

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:50.670
in most cases.

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:54.230
We are trying to relieve that
pressure by taking control

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:59.460
back over our clusters through
the ephemeral cluster design.

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:00.740
Make sense?

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:02.420
It's logical, right?

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:05.540
Now what do you get with this?

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:07.190
First thing that you
get is the ability

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:11.870
to have very controlled
configurations--

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:13.640
much finer control
over the jobs.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:20.210
Second is I can actually
scale my cluster independently

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:24.830
off any other job that I might
be sharing in a shared cluster

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.710
architecture.

00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:30.950
So I could deploy 1,000
nodes for one job,

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:33.780
whereas my cluster itself
was 100 nodes earlier.

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.782
Huge value unlocking.

00:13:38.782 --> 00:13:39.490
Let's keep going.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.230
Job specific cluster.

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:45.820
How does it work?

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:50.260
I start my job through Cloud
Dataproc API or through the UI.

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:51.820
It doesn't matter.

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:57.250
It spins up a cluster for you,
which you configured earlier.

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:58.750
The job runs.

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:00.040
It generates data.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:02.440
In this case, I'm
actually outputting data

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:04.780
to Google BigQuery--

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:05.890
Cloud BigQuery.

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:08.140
And we'll talk about
why that is so.

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:12.250
I also am storing
data in Cloud Storage.

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:15.030
I run my job, and once
the job is complete,

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:17.950
I actually delete the cluster.

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:19.510
I delete the cluster.

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:20.837
Can you believe it?

00:14:20.837 --> 00:14:21.920
Because this is a problem.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:27.900
We talked about this
diagram earlier.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:30.850
That was our simple
starting point.

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:32.930
Life is never simple.

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:36.410
Simple diagrams don't actually
do justice to the problems

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:38.670
we have to face
in the real world.

00:14:38.670 --> 00:14:42.550
Let's build on
this a little bit.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:45.512
This is my migration end state--

00:14:45.512 --> 00:14:49.490
the cloud-native solution.

00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:53.390
On the left, in green, you
see my ephemeral clusters.

00:14:53.390 --> 00:14:54.550
We just talked about them.

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:55.670
Job specific.

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:56.810
I can have a bunch of them.

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:58.260
I don't need 1, 2, 3.

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:02.030
I could have hundreds of
them if need be, as required.

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:06.560
In the middle, in blue, you
see my different data stores.

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:09.950
I already talked
about Cloud Storage.

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:12.770
I'm adding two more to this mix.

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:17.030
It's BigQuery and Bigtable.

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:20.450
BigQuery, for those of you
who do not know about it,

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:23.360
is a terabyte scale
data warehouse,

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:27.260
and Bigtable is really
about managing sparse data

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:30.200
that you want to read and
write to very, very fast.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:34.030
Very low latency read
and write access.

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:35.826
Both of those are
now available for me.

00:15:35.826 --> 00:15:37.450
Remember, I was
talking about unlocking

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:39.400
that value stored in HDFS.

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:41.330
That's what we are doing.

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:45.270
We also are talking about
monitoring and logging.

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:48.520
Get a little bit better
idea of your operations

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:54.320
and what you need to tune as you
actually get data from runtime.

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:59.240
Below my ephemeral clusters,
you see an orchestration engine.

00:15:59.240 --> 00:16:02.760
You see Apache Airflow.

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:07.270
Apache Airflow or a
variant like an Azkaban--

00:16:07.270 --> 00:16:10.150
that's what you can
implement for coordinating

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:14.100
the flow of your jobs
and their dependencies.

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:18.090
We actually have
recently GA'd a product

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:22.670
called Google Cloud Composer
and James has a very, very big

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:24.480
section in the afternoon today.

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.580
You want to give some
details about that session?

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:29.970
JAMES MALONE: Yeah, so
there's a session at 1:55

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:32.850
on our GA launch of
Cloud Composer, which

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:34.990
is a managed Apache
Airflow service.

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:37.350
And the goal with having
a managed Apache Airflow

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:39.720
service is to solve
the cloud level

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:42.390
workflows and orchestrations,
but have a product that's

00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:44.700
open source that can talk
to things inside of Google,

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:46.295
talk to things
outside of Google.

00:16:46.295 --> 00:16:47.670
In that session,
we'll be talking

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:49.950
about why we launched
Composer, kind of our vision

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:50.994
for Composer.

00:16:50.994 --> 00:16:52.410
If you're new to
Airflow, we'll be

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:55.770
breaking apart an Airflow DAG
and giving a demo of Composer.

00:16:55.770 --> 00:16:58.890
So it's a good kind
of baseline and intro

00:16:58.890 --> 00:17:02.370
to Airflow and Cloud
Composer, if you're curious.

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:05.940
And it's at 1:55 today, and
I think it's not quite full,

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:08.140
but it is almost at capacity.

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:11.329
So if you're curious,
do sign up for it.

00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:12.480
RAJEEV MAHAJAN: OK.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:17.089
And now, in yellow,
on the right--

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:19.290
yellow or orange--
whatever you're seeing--

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:25.424
I have persistent clusters.

00:17:25.424 --> 00:17:26.470
What?

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:27.970
Didn't I just tell
you that you need

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:29.860
to go over the ephemeral
clusters stateless,

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:32.193
and I'm telling you you need
to have persistent clusters

00:17:32.193 --> 00:17:33.550
in this architecture?

00:17:33.550 --> 00:17:34.780
Life is complex.

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:36.970
We'll talk about it in
a couple of seconds.

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:42.990
Say you have a
Spark streaming job.

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:50.110
Running it on an ephemeral
cluster is next to impossible.

00:17:50.110 --> 00:17:52.090
You need a persistent cluster.

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:54.270
You may also have
a use case where

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:59.340
you have a bunch of different
jobs that need to run together.

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:01.920
They're dependent on each
other, and some of these jobs

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:03.730
actually are very short run.

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:06.390
They might just be ingesting
data into the cluster itself

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:09.060
or into GCS from a source.

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:11.250
The overhead of
spinning up a cluster

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:14.310
might be much larger than
you want to tolerate.

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:16.830
That's also a good
case for running

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:18.380
maybe a short and
persistent cluster,

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:21.000
but a persistent
cluster, none the less.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:22.180
Make sense?

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:24.800
But if you are going to
run a persistent cluster,

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:26.770
here are a couple
of recommendations.

00:18:26.770 --> 00:18:31.110
Number one, scope it to be
the smallest possible cluster

00:18:31.110 --> 00:18:34.170
that you can think of, in
terms of number of jobs

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:38.100
you're going to run and the
number of nodes that are going

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:39.800
to be part of that cluster.

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:45.610
It's a very, very
simple recommendation.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:47.680
So we talked about the
underlying components,

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:49.840
we talked about
architectural principles.

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:51.160
Let's get to the mechanics.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:59.130
First, move data to GCS.

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:00.962
Again, I'm repeating it again.

00:19:00.962 --> 00:19:03.420
Do it incrementally, and we'll
actually talk about a couple

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:06.440
of strategies in a
couple of minutes.

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:08.690
Second thing is experiment.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:12.710
What does that mean?

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:16.160
Now that I have actually opened
up the architecture for you,

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:19.460
I can run job specific
ephemeral clusters.

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:22.110
I want you to run
proofs of concept.

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:24.510
I want you to experiment with
the size of your cluster,

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:26.810
the number of nodes--

00:19:26.810 --> 00:19:28.820
try out different
configurations.

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:34.070
Figure out what works best for
you, in terms of the dollars

00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:35.540
that you are
spending, the time it

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:37.480
takes to run a particular
job, because that's

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:38.930
a requirement in many cases.

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:41.870
I want to go from 12
hours to two hours.

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:43.320
That's my new window.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:45.260
You can experiment
and you can actually

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:47.200
make it happen with
Cloud Dataproc.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:49.550
So that's our recommendation.

00:19:49.550 --> 00:19:51.890
Central to all of this
is ephemeral clusters,

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:54.770
which we have been talking
about over and over again.

00:19:58.110 --> 00:19:59.590
Migrating incrementally.

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:00.870
OK, so what's new?

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:03.300
Anybody who has been
in the world of IT

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:07.440
thinks in terms of eating
the elephant in small chunks,

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:08.250
right?

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:11.490
The only guidance up here
is when you are actually

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:16.200
creating POCs or you're defining
the scope of your Agile Sprint,

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:19.170
if that's your thing,
then think in terms

00:20:19.170 --> 00:20:21.300
of dependencies of the jobs.

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:22.560
Move them together.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:26.190
It will actually make
things simple for you.

00:20:26.190 --> 00:20:30.240
Remember, what ephemeral
clusters are are stateless.

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:32.940
You have input,
you have output--

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:35.220
there's nothing inside
the cluster, itself.

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:38.580
It runs, it dies, it
rises up like the Phoenix,

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:40.590
and it dies again.

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.090
The cycle of birth
and rebirth keeps

00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:44.400
on going on with an
ephemeral cluster, right?

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:49.270
Think incrementally.

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:52.390
Let's take, actually, a
look at an architecture

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:55.310
which will help you do that.

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:58.646
This architecture is something
called a backfill pipeline.

00:20:58.646 --> 00:20:59.770
Are you guys aware of this?

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:00.940
Have you ever heard of this?

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:01.780
Anybody?

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:02.500
Yes?

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:04.810
OK.

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:07.780
So say you have your
big data stack running

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:09.970
for the last three years.

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:12.280
Recently, in the last
six months or so,

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:16.090
you created a bunch of new jobs.

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:19.550
But since your cluster is
already running to capacity,

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:22.410
you'll never have the
time to go back and run

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:28.140
these jobs against your archive
data from 2 and 1/2 years ago.

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:32.580
The idea of a backfill pipeline
is to actually take your jobs

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:37.010
and run it against archive data.

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:39.640
So how is that relevant to us?

00:21:39.640 --> 00:21:42.550
Remember, I've been
saying experiment.

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:46.060
Using your jobs-- the
newly migrated jobs--

00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:49.000
against archive data is
actually a very good way

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:52.210
of validating that your
jobs will run well,

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:55.946
they will run correct, and you
will get the characteristics

00:21:55.946 --> 00:21:57.820
you are looking for, in
terms of performance,

00:21:57.820 --> 00:21:59.890
times, et cetera, et cetera.

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:01.620
Make sense?

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:04.430
So this is one low
risk architecture.

00:22:04.430 --> 00:22:06.650
The key component up
here is that little box

00:22:06.650 --> 00:22:09.200
in the middle, which says
internet gateway and cloud

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:10.040
storage.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:15.140
We are trying to bring data over
from your on-premise cluster

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:16.739
into Cloud Dataproc.

00:22:16.739 --> 00:22:18.530
That's the key idea
there, and are actually

00:22:18.530 --> 00:22:21.050
going to expand on this
a little bit further, OK?

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:28.690
So you are in the
middle of a migration.

00:22:28.690 --> 00:22:33.107
Before migration, you had
your on-premise cluster.

00:22:33.107 --> 00:22:35.440
After migration, you have
that cloud-native architecture

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:36.880
I talked about.

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:41.020
Somewhere in the middle, both
of them are living together.

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:43.000
That's the idea behind
hybrid architecture.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:44.870
Nothing new there.

00:22:44.870 --> 00:22:48.670
Now this hybrid architecture
we just talked about

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:52.060
is being imposed on you by
the requirements of being

00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:54.610
in the middle of
a migration cycle,

00:22:54.610 --> 00:22:57.490
but there might be business
requirements for you

00:22:57.490 --> 00:23:00.670
to actually have a
hybrid architecture.

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:02.900
I'll give you an example.

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:07.810
One of my customers is actually
running their order management

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:11.380
system on-premise.

00:23:11.380 --> 00:23:13.870
They're looking to
migrate it to the cloud,

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:17.050
but the problem is it's going
to take a little bit of time

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:19.210
for them to figure out
what that looks like.

00:23:19.210 --> 00:23:20.480
It's a monolithic system.

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:22.229
They need to figure
out how to create

00:23:22.229 --> 00:23:23.770
micro services, et
cetera, et cetera,

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:27.350
and do that
appropriate migration.

00:23:27.350 --> 00:23:31.180
So they want to, for
the foreseeable future,

00:23:31.180 --> 00:23:33.490
run their extraction
and big data

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:38.570
jobs relating to order
management system in-house.

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:43.130
That's the hybrid architecture
we are talking about.

00:23:43.130 --> 00:23:47.450
Example, again, ephemeral
clusters in green on the left.

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:50.050
We have the storage
in blue and we have

00:23:50.050 --> 00:23:52.410
on-premise yellow on the right.

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:56.350
And in the middle
is a contraption

00:23:56.350 --> 00:23:59.980
for bringing your data over
from your on-premise clusters

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:03.850
over into Cloud Storage.

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:08.080
VPN gateways are involved
and some more clusters

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:08.770
are involved.

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:12.340
Now these clusters are for
actually copying the data over

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:15.610
from your on-premise cluster
over to cloud storage.

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:18.160
There are a couple
of mechanisms there.

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:20.770
It's really out of the scope of
this particular presentation,

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:22.960
but we will point you
to a couple of links

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:25.960
where you can actually go and
take a closer look at them.

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:28.210
And maybe this is something
where you actually

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:31.291
engage Google professional
services to help you with.

00:24:31.291 --> 00:24:31.790
OK.

00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:34.900
There's a push and pull kind
of an architecture involved.

00:24:40.420 --> 00:24:43.960
Of the three recommendations
I gave you in the beginning,

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:47.170
the first one was look
at a broader portfolio

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:49.750
of Google Cloud Services.

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:52.390
Let's talk about what are
the considerations there.

00:24:54.841 --> 00:24:55.340
Regions.

00:24:57.970 --> 00:25:01.630
Say you are deploying
new architecture.

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:03.630
It's a multi-regional
architecture,

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:06.640
and for some reason, your
persistent data in Google Cloud

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:10.000
Storage is sitting in a region
which is separate from where

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:11.960
your resources are located--

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:15.460
your ephemeral clusters
are located, et cetera.

00:25:15.460 --> 00:25:17.620
What is probably going
to happen for you

00:25:17.620 --> 00:25:22.060
is Google Cloud is probably
going to, in certain cases,

00:25:22.060 --> 00:25:25.420
copy the data over from
one region to another.

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:27.400
There are latency
considerations and there

00:25:27.400 --> 00:25:29.980
are cost considerations
because of egress costs,

00:25:29.980 --> 00:25:32.660
and you need to think
through some of that,

00:25:32.660 --> 00:25:38.208
especially if you are building
any kind of DR kind of a setup.

00:25:38.208 --> 00:25:40.630
OK.

00:25:40.630 --> 00:25:43.150
All Google Cloud services
have Stackdriver logging

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:44.920
and monitoring built into them.

00:25:44.920 --> 00:25:48.706
You can use your
on-premise or whatever tool

00:25:48.706 --> 00:25:50.080
you are using to
bring that data,

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:52.120
or you can actually
use Stackdriver.

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:53.650
We think it's
pretty cool, so you

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:57.890
should stay with Stackdriver,
but it's your choice.

00:25:57.890 --> 00:26:00.550
Finally, I have talked
about this multiple times--

00:26:00.550 --> 00:26:06.850
we can store data in BigQuery
and in Bigtable as needed.

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:07.570
Section 2.

00:26:07.570 --> 00:26:09.130
So we've talked
about infrastructure.

00:26:09.130 --> 00:26:10.588
Let's talk very
quickly about data.

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:15.470
We've been through
this a few times.

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:19.330
You can migrate data from
Apache HBase to Bigtable--

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:20.890
equivalent technologies.

00:26:20.890 --> 00:26:25.090
You can migrate data from
Apache Impala to BigQuery,

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:27.430
and, if you've got
Hive running, you

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:30.770
may want to think about running
your Hive metastore on Cloud

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:37.011
SQL, which is Google's open
source implementation of mySQL.

00:26:37.011 --> 00:26:39.990
Make sense?

00:26:39.990 --> 00:26:42.590
Security.

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:46.100
You are never going to get
the level of granular control

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:51.530
that you get in HDFS, but
using Google's IAM policies,

00:26:51.530 --> 00:26:55.650
you can have equivalent
control over the data that is

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:58.460
stored in Google Cloud Storage.

00:26:58.460 --> 00:27:00.500
We are recommending
that you apply controls

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:04.680
at the level of buckets,
not at the level of files.

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:07.530
But if, for some
reason, your use case

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:11.240
requires that you work
at the level of a file,

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:16.131
then think in terms
of using GCS ACLs.

00:27:16.131 --> 00:27:18.630
We don't recommend it, but if
you have to go down that path,

00:27:18.630 --> 00:27:19.971
you have to go down that path.

00:27:19.971 --> 00:27:20.933
Make sense?

00:27:26.230 --> 00:27:28.180
Speed.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:31.770
I process terabytes
of data per data,

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:36.880
and my VPN connection
is not fast enough

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:38.920
or doesn't have
enough bandwidth.

00:27:38.920 --> 00:27:40.270
What do I do?

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:43.169
So some people deploy
multiple VPN connections.

00:27:43.169 --> 00:27:44.710
That might solve
the problem for you,

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:48.490
depending on the volume of
data you are working with.

00:27:48.490 --> 00:27:52.150
Or you may want to look at
two alternative technologies

00:27:52.150 --> 00:27:54.750
to plug into Google's network--

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:56.020
Direct Peering.

00:27:56.020 --> 00:27:58.690
Have you guys been familiar
with Direct Peering?

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:00.350
Anybody deploy it?

00:28:00.350 --> 00:28:02.890
Direct Peering is
essentially your ability

00:28:02.890 --> 00:28:07.440
to tap into Google's
fast network directly

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:10.340
through the point of presence--

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:11.215
what do we call them?

00:28:11.215 --> 00:28:12.660
POPs?

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:16.960
POP setups.

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:20.390
If you are unable to
do that for any reason,

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:22.270
you can actually use
our partner services

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:24.110
called Cloud
Interconnect services,

00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:27.400
where a partner actually lays
the cable from their data

00:28:27.400 --> 00:28:30.340
center, which is kind
of located and connects

00:28:30.340 --> 00:28:32.230
into Google network.

00:28:32.230 --> 00:28:35.360
And you can broaden
that bandwidth.

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:36.280
Make sense?

00:28:36.280 --> 00:28:38.410
So these are the key
elements for data.

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:41.920
It's bandwidth,
it's access control,

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:45.370
and where you are storing data.

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:48.230
Let's talk about jobs.

00:28:48.230 --> 00:28:49.900
Remember, the first
thing I said was

00:28:49.900 --> 00:28:52.660
if all you are looking
to do is to migrate

00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:56.260
your jobs from on-premise
to Cloud Dataproc,

00:28:56.260 --> 00:28:57.700
it's very, very simple to do.

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:02.800
HDFS-- those four letters--

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:05.470
you replace it with
those two letters, GS.

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:09.530
That's all you need to do.

00:29:09.530 --> 00:29:10.481
It's pretty cool, huh?

00:29:15.210 --> 00:29:19.000
We support-- these
come as default setups,

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:22.210
but we are not limited to
actually supporting these.

00:29:22.210 --> 00:29:25.660
So we have something called
initialization actions,

00:29:25.660 --> 00:29:30.800
where you can define what
kind of stack you want to run,

00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:34.340
and it will add runtime,
deploy the stack for you,

00:29:34.340 --> 00:29:36.070
and you can continue
with your running

00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:38.110
that particular stack or jobs.

00:29:38.110 --> 00:29:40.900
So these are just the
default configurations

00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:44.470
available to you, but
initialization actions lets you

00:29:44.470 --> 00:29:47.072
open up the whole stack, right?

00:29:47.072 --> 00:29:48.280
JAMES MALONE: That's correct.

00:29:48.280 --> 00:29:50.380
Something that's
not on here that's

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:53.980
a feature that we are
releasing in the next few weeks

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:55.570
is the ability to
also pick and choose

00:29:55.570 --> 00:29:57.694
which components you would
like on your cluster.

00:29:57.694 --> 00:29:59.860
So canonically right now,
when you create a cluster,

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:02.257
you can specify one or more
initialization actions.

00:30:02.257 --> 00:30:04.840
And those initialization actions
are shell scripts or anything

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:07.900
that can be executed in
a Debian environment.

00:30:07.900 --> 00:30:09.280
People have asked
for the ability

00:30:09.280 --> 00:30:10.600
to do things like tick a box.

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:13.210
So I want a cluster
with Spark and maybe add

00:30:13.210 --> 00:30:15.520
Zeppelin or HBase.

00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:16.990
So in the coming
weeks, you'll see

00:30:16.990 --> 00:30:18.260
when you create a
Dataproc cluster,

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:20.551
there'll be additional options
and you just say, create

00:30:20.551 --> 00:30:23.170
a cluster with Zeppelin,
Anaconda, Livy,

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:24.570
whatever else you want.

00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:26.320
That makes it easier
to create the cluster

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:27.729
with the optional components.

00:30:27.729 --> 00:30:29.270
It also means, on
the back end, we're

00:30:29.270 --> 00:30:32.200
testing all of those components
against each Dataproc image

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:33.280
build.

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:35.260
That's probably a
session in and of itself

00:30:35.260 --> 00:30:37.468
because how we build the
image is really interesting,

00:30:37.468 --> 00:30:39.030
but quite a lengthy process.

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:41.530
But it also means that we're
testing all of those components

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:44.071
against our weekly image builds
to make sure that they work--

00:30:44.071 --> 00:30:47.184
that things don't break with
each new release of Debian,

00:30:47.184 --> 00:30:49.100
each new release of the
open source components

00:30:49.100 --> 00:30:52.370
and all of their dependencies,
which can be quite complicated.

00:30:52.370 --> 00:30:53.925
So it's abstracting
away quite a bit

00:30:53.925 --> 00:30:55.300
of that complexity
for you so you

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:56.508
don't have to worry about it.

00:31:01.517 --> 00:31:03.100
RAJEEV MAHAJAN: The
key point I wanted

00:31:03.100 --> 00:31:05.930
to actually bubble up
in this particular slide

00:31:05.930 --> 00:31:06.910
are two things.

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:10.360
One, as you migrate from
your on-premise cluster

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:12.430
over to ephemeral
clusters, you may

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:15.552
want to start with the smallest
possible configuration that

00:31:15.552 --> 00:31:17.260
is closest to what
you were running with,

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:18.940
and then do the experiments.

00:31:18.940 --> 00:31:23.230
You might find that even
with a smaller configuration,

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:25.240
you might get
equivalent performance.

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:26.686
You'll be surprised there.

00:31:26.686 --> 00:31:28.060
The second point
I wanted to make

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:31.900
was this point about preemptible
instances or preemptible

00:31:31.900 --> 00:31:33.710
workers.

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:37.430
Preemptible workers is
really a cool feature,

00:31:37.430 --> 00:31:43.000
wherein you can take
discounts of up to 80%

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:46.990
on the regular pricing
on instances that Google,

00:31:46.990 --> 00:31:50.620
if needed be, can reclaim
back by telling you

00:31:50.620 --> 00:31:52.730
that we are actually
claiming these instances.

00:31:52.730 --> 00:31:57.280
But if not, you can run some
of your higher latency jobs--

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:00.244
something that is not critical
at a lower price point.

00:32:00.244 --> 00:32:01.660
They are very,
very cool features.

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:09.570
Final piece is all
about scalability.

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:13.690
Cloud Dataproc allows you
to scale both up and down,

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:19.720
and we also have this concept
of graceful degradation.

00:32:19.720 --> 00:32:22.470
So we actually let
the cluster figure out

00:32:22.470 --> 00:32:24.870
that it's ready to
scale up or down--

00:32:24.870 --> 00:32:28.020
usually down at the right
moment without crashing nodes

00:32:28.020 --> 00:32:30.150
and creating problems for you.

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:33.794
Last piece in this
is restartable jobs.

00:32:33.794 --> 00:32:37.170
Dataproc also has
the intelligence

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:39.540
to figure out if a job
has actually completed

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:43.440
or it was aborted for a
plethora of different reasons.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:45.720
Maybe some services
were not available

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:48.330
or you had some
dependencies that you

00:32:48.330 --> 00:32:50.650
couldn't do anything about.

00:32:50.650 --> 00:32:53.670
So you may want to think
about building your jobs

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:56.870
or modifying them slightly,
so they are restartable.

00:32:56.870 --> 00:32:58.860
Make sense?

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:03.570
For longer running jobs--
say a Spark job that

00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:08.370
runs for 20 hours or so,
what you may want to do

00:33:08.370 --> 00:33:12.970
is use preemptible instances,
but build in your own variation

00:33:12.970 --> 00:33:14.190
of checkpointing in it.

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:17.850
So at the appropriate time, save
the data into Cloud Storage,

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:20.790
and if, for some reason,
the instances get preempted

00:33:20.790 --> 00:33:22.470
and you have to
restart, you still

00:33:22.470 --> 00:33:26.370
haven't lost 15 hours
of work that you did.

00:33:26.370 --> 00:33:28.470
It's not complicated to do.

00:33:28.470 --> 00:33:31.240
It's very simple, and if any of
you guys want to talk about it,

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:32.842
we can do that offline.

00:33:32.842 --> 00:33:36.290
Make sense?

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:38.960
Now you've got the
migration done.

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:42.230
We are wanting to run
this on an ongoing basis.

00:33:42.230 --> 00:33:44.900
What you may want to do
is to apply custom labels

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:47.600
for identifying your
clusters, cost charge backs,

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:48.860
et cetera, et cetera.

00:33:48.860 --> 00:33:52.220
This is just a basic feature
built into all of Google Cloud

00:33:52.220 --> 00:33:53.710
Platform.

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:55.520
We've already talked
about organizing

00:33:55.520 --> 00:34:01.040
GCS buckets to separate jobs
and applying access controls.

00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:04.520
And finally, we've talked
about ephemeral clusters

00:34:04.520 --> 00:34:08.330
and the ability to run one
or closely related jobs

00:34:08.330 --> 00:34:09.350
on the same cluster.

00:34:09.350 --> 00:34:11.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

